<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>SvxLink DB9CR APT repository</title>
@@HREFLANG_LINKS@@
<style>
  body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; max-width: 46rem; margin: 2rem auto; padding: 0 1rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  pre { background: #f0f0f0; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; overflow-x: auto; border-radius: 4px; }
  code { background: #f0f0f0; padding: 0.1rem 0.3rem; border-radius: 3px; }
  pre code { background: none; padding: 0; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1rem 0; }
  th, td { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 0.3rem 0.6rem; text-align: left; }
  .note { color: #555; font-size: 0.95em; }
  .langs { font-size: 0.85em; line-height: 1.8; border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc; padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .langs a, .langs strong { white-space: nowrap; margin-right: 0.6em; }
  .ai-notice { background: #fff4ce; border: 1px solid #e0c060; border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
@@LANG_SWITCHER@@
<h1>SvxLink DB9CR APT repository</h1>
@@AI_NOTICE@@
<p>Packages of
<a href="https://github.com/@@REPO_SLUG@@">svxlink-db9cr</a> for Raspberry Pi OS
and Debian, in two components &mdash; pick one:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>main</code>: daily development builds from the latest source of
this fork, not stable releases.</li>
<li><code>release</code>: the official upstream SvxLink releases (26.05 and
newer), built from the unmodified upstream release source with the same
build settings and features as the daily packages. Every release is kept;
<code>apt upgrade</code> moves to each new upstream release.</li>
</ul>
<p>See the <a href="https://github.com/@@REPO_SLUG@@">source repository</a>
for details.</p>

<h2>Upstream &amp; local changes</h2>
<p>This is a daily-synced fork of
<a href="https://github.com/sm0svx/svxlink">sm0svx/svxlink</a>, the
original SvxLink project, with local packaging and feature additions
layered on top (RTL-SDR, SipLogic, the separate <code>qtel</code> package,
this APT repository, and more &mdash; see
<a href="https://github.com/@@REPO_SLUG@@/blob/master/CHANGELOG.MD">CHANGELOG.MD</a>
for the full list). To see exactly what is different from upstream at any
point in time, without relying on a snapshot here going stale, compare
live on GitHub:
<a href="https://github.com/sm0svx/svxlink/compare/master...criede:svxlink-db9cr:master">upstream master&nbsp;&hellip;&nbsp;this fork's master</a>.</p>

<h2>Quickstart</h2>
<p>Run on the target system (detects the right codename/architecture automatically):</p>
<pre><code># Import the public signing key
curl -fsSL https://criede.github.io/svxlink-db9cr/pubkey.gpg | \
  sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/svxlink-db9cr.gpg

# Add the repository source. Choose the component:
#   main     daily development builds
#   release  official upstream releases (26.05 and newer)
component=main
codename=$(sed -n 's/^VERSION_CODENAME=//p' /etc/os-release | tr -d '"')
arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture)
echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/svxlink-db9cr.gpg arch=${arch}] https://criede.github.io/svxlink-db9cr ${codename} ${component}" | \
  sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/svxlink-db9cr.list

sudo apt update
sudo apt install svxlink

# Mandatory before the first start: configure your station, radio and audio.
# The packaged file is only a template and must be adapted to your setup.
sudo editor /etc/svxlink/svxlink.conf

# Enable SvxLink at boot and start it now.
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now svxlink.service

# Check service state and follow its log.
systemctl status svxlink.service
journalctl -u svxlink.service -f

# Optional graphical EchoLink client (needs a desktop environment):
# sudo apt install qtel</code></pre>
<p class="note"><strong>Required:</strong> Do not start the service before
editing <code>/etc/svxlink/svxlink.conf</code>. Configure at least the station
identity, logic, receiver/transmitter and audio/PTT settings for your hardware.
The service reads this path through <code>/etc/default/svxlink</code>.</p>
<p class="note">If <code>apt update</code> reports a 404 for this source, your
system's codename isn't built yet (see the table below) &mdash; edit the
codename in <code>/etc/apt/sources.list.d/svxlink-db9cr.list</code> to one of
the supported ones instead. Afterwards, a regular <code>sudo apt upgrade</code>
picks up new daily builds (<code>main</code>) or new upstream releases
(<code>release</code>) automatically.</p>
<p class="note">Do not enable both components at once unless you want the
daily builds: they always have the higher version, so apt would install them
over the releases. To switch an existing installation from <code>main</code>
to <code>release</code>, change the component in
<code>/etc/apt/sources.list.d/svxlink-db9cr.list</code>, run
<code>sudo apt update</code>, then install the release version explicitly
(it is a downgrade), e.g.
<code>sudo apt install svxlink=26.05.2-1~trixie</code>. Note that in
<code>release</code> packages (unmodified upstream source) the TCL scripts
under <code>/usr/share/svxlink</code> are not protected as configuration
files; keep local changes in <code>events.d/local/</code>.</p>

<h2>Applying configuration changes</h2>
<p>After editing <code>/etc/svxlink/svxlink.conf</code> (or any
<code>/etc/svxlink/svxlink.d/*.conf</code> module file) on a running
station, reload it without dropping an active session:</p>
<pre><code>sudo systemctl reload svxlink.service</code></pre>
<p class="note">This sends <code>SIGHUP</code>, which SvxLink re-reads its
configuration on. Use <code>sudo systemctl restart svxlink.service</code>
instead if a reload does not seem to pick up a change (some settings, such
as which logic cores or sound devices are loaded, may need a full restart).</p>

<h2>Installing an older version</h2>
<p>Recent versions stay available in the pool for a while after being
superseded (daily for a week, weekly for a month, monthly for a year &mdash;
see "Retention" in
<a href="https://github.com/@@REPO_SLUG@@/blob/master/.github/UPSTREAM-SYNC.MD">UPSTREAM-SYNC.MD</a>),
so a bad daily build can usually be rolled back. Upstream release packages
(<code>release</code> component) are never removed:</p>
<pre><code>apt list -a svxlink   # or qtel; lists every version still in the repository
sudo apt install svxlink=26.05.1+daily20260910.5.1.gabcdef012345-1~trixie
sudo apt-mark hold svxlink   # optional: stop "apt upgrade" pulling it forward again
# sudo apt-mark unhold svxlink   # later, to resume automatic updates</code></pre>

<h2>Packages</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Package</th><th>Contains</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>svxlink</code></td><td>Server components (svxlink, remotetrx, svxreflector, logic cores, Tcl event scripts). Built with: RTL-SDR direct-USB support (bundled RTL-SDR Blog librtlsdr/rtl_tcp fork for current V3/V4 dongles; conflicts with the distro librtlsdr0/rtl-sdr), the contributed SipLogic logic core (bundled PJSIP/pjproject, statically linked), GPIO via libgpiod, and Speex/Opus/LADSPA audio codec and plugin support. Installs a systemd service. Does not include Qtel or sound packs.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qtel</code></td><td>Graphical EchoLink client (Qt6). Requires a desktop environment and the <code>svxlink</code> package of the exact same version (shares its echolib/async libraries).</td></tr>
@@EXTRA_PACKAGE_ROWS@@</table>

<h2>Supported systems</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Codename</th><th>Matches</th><th>Architectures: <code>main</code> (daily)</th><th>Architectures: <code>release</code></th></tr>
@@SUITE_ROWS@@</table>
<p class="note"><strong>32-bit systems:</strong> <code>armhf</code> packages
are built against Raspbian (ARMv6+VFP), the base of 32-bit Raspberry Pi OS,
so they run on every Raspberry Pi model including the Pi&nbsp;1, Zero and
Zero&nbsp;W, and also install on Debian <code>armhf</code> (both are tested).
<code>i386</code> (32-bit PCs) is built for Debian&nbsp;12 only: Debian&nbsp;13
no longer supports installing i386 systems. The 32-bit packages contain the
same features as the 64-bit ones.</p>

<h2>Signing key</h2>
<p><a href="pubkey.gpg">pubkey.gpg</a> &mdash; imported automatically by the
quickstart commands above.</p>

<h2>Browse repository files</h2>
<p><a href="dists/">dists/</a> (per-suite indices, Release files, Packages
lists) &middot; <a href="pool/">pool/</a> (the actual <code>.deb</code>
files) &middot; <a href="https://github.com/@@REPO_SLUG@@">source
repository</a> on GitHub.</p>
</body>
</html>
